<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Illyria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Illyria was the name given by ancient Greek and Roman writers to the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> along the eastern Adriatic, home of the Illyrian peoples. Its kingdoms, notably that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardylis">Bardylis</a> and that of the Ardiaean dynasty under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentius">Gentius</a>, fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> until the Roman conquest of 168 BCE. The region became the Roman province of Illyricum, and the name was revived for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/illyria.jpg" alt="Illyria" width="320" height="291" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ahmet Q. · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Illyrians_in_the_1st-2nd_centuries_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Illyria was a region in the western part of the Balkan Peninsula, named for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrians">Illyrians</a>, a group of Indo-European speaking peoples who inhabited it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> first used Illyris for roughly northern and central present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, down to the valley of the Aoös, the modern Vjosa, and the Bay of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a>, neighbouring Macedonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. In Roman times Illyria, Illyris and Illyricum came to mean a broader region reaching from the south-eastern Adriatic coast, modern Albania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, and its hinterland to the whole eastern Adriatic and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest recorded Illyrian kingdom was that of the Enchele in the 8th century BCE, and the era of other kingdoms runs from about 400 BCE. The Autariatae under Pleurias, in 337 BCE, are counted as one. The most notable were those of Bardyllis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardani</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agron">Agron</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardiaei">Ardiaei</a>, who created the last and best-known Illyrian kingdom. The kingdoms were small and covered only parts of Illyria. Polybius describes peasant infantry fighting under aristocrats, each holding a town, and the monarchy passed by descent, with marriages used as alliances. In the south the kingdoms imitated their Greek neighbours.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teuta">Teuta</a> in the First Illyrian War. She withdrew to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-kotor">Bay of Kotor</a>, and in 228 BCE Rome imposed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-dalmatia">Issa</a> and Corcyra and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durres">Epidamnos</a>, Apollonia and Oricum. Macedon tried without success to take the southern Illyrian ports as bases for an attack upon the Italian Peninsula. In 168 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentius">Gentius</a>, the last king, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Scodra</a>, and took him to Rome in 165 BCE. Four client republics were set up, and the region was later governed directly as a province with Scodra as its capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Roman province of Illyricum stretched from the river Drilon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> to Istria and the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a>, near modern Split. After a revolt of Pannonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daesitiates">Daesitiates</a> the province was divided in 10 CE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> in the south, but the name Illyria stayed in use in Late Latin and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. In the Late Roman Empire it named a praetorian prefecture.</p><p>Church history kept the name too. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> appointed papal vicars for Illyricum, and in 515 forty Illyrian bishops renewed their loyalty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> under Pope Hormisdas; in the 8th century the patriarchs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> brought Illyria under their jurisdiction. Jewish communities are attested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narona">Narona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, known from menorahs and inscriptions.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Illyria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom first lay along the Haliacmon and Loudias rivers north of Mount Olympus and along the coast of the Thermaic Gulf, bordered by Epirus, <b>Illyria</b>, Paeonia, Thrace and Thessaly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The poet Valentin Vodnik celebrated the change in <b>Illyria</b> Revived.</blockquote><a href="/state/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperor kept the title King of <b>Illyria</b> until 1918.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-illyria">Kingdom of Illyria</a></li><li><blockquote>Ancient writers traced them to <b>Illyria</b> or Crete; modern scholars note links across the Adriatic but see a largely local formation.</blockquote><a href="/state/messapians">Messapians</a></li></ul></section>
